title: A migrate command: import a tracker from beads and its like
created: 2026-09-18T17:33:43Z
updated: 2026-09-18T17:33:43Z
effort: L
area: cli
handoff:

Someone with history in another tracker cannot try pidly without abandoning
it. An import is the difference between "interesting" and "usable on a real
project", and 6 needs one anyway — it says to migrate any existing bd data
and does not say how.

Both open and closed tickets, because closed ones are the reason anybody
keeps a tracker. In pidly a closed ticket is a directory under c/, so this is
not an extra mode: it is where the file gets written.

The shape of the problem, measured on ../idol, which 38 exists to study and
is the only real corpus available:

  342 records in .beads/issues.jsonl
    332 issues: 278 closed, 51 open, 1 in_progress, 2 deferred
     10 memory: a key/value store with no pidly equivalent at all
  keys present: id title description status priority issue_type assignee
    owner created_at updated_at started_at closed_at close_reason
    acceptance_criteria (70) notes (128) dependencies (115) labels (33)
    design (6)

What maps cleanly:

  title          -> title:
  description    -> the body
  created_at     -> created:
  updated_at     -> updated:
  open/closed    -> the path, c/ or not
  dependencies   -> needs:, for the depends-on kind
  parent-child   -> the directory, since a child ticket lives inside its parent

What does not, and these are the decisions:

- ids. beads ids are slugs — idol-jay, idol-xdyn.12.1 — and pidly numbers are
  integers that are never reused. So every ticket is renumbered, every
  dependency has to be rewritten to the new numbers, and the old id has to
  land somewhere or the history stops matching what commit messages and
  external links say. A field, or a line in the body. The registry exists to
  stop a number being issued twice and an import must fill it.
- in_progress. pidly has no such state: a ticket is claimed or it is not, and
  a claim names a process that no longer exists. Import as unclaimed, and say
  so, because a claim nobody can verify is the one thing the format refuses
  to invent.
- deferred. pidly has defer:, so this one maps, but beads carries no trigger
  and a deferred ticket without a trigger is what 42 rejected.
- priority, issue_type, assignee, labels. Every one is a project field the
  importing project may or may not declare, and labels is a set, which is 35
  and deliberately unsolved. An import cannot add fields to a schema it did
  not write, so it either declines them, writes them into the body, or asks
  the schema to be extended first.
- acceptance_criteria, notes, design. Free text with no home, and 37 decided
  the body has no structure. They concatenate under headings, or they are
  dropped and the import says how many it dropped.
- memory records. No equivalent, and inventing one is out of scope. Report
  and skip.

Which tools, in the order they are worth doing. Only the first is grounded in
anything here; the rest are guesses about what people use and want checking
against real exports before a line of code:

- beads. The one the project already has to leave, with a corpus to test on.
- GitHub issues, via gh issue list --json. The largest population, and an
  export anyone can produce without a new dependency.
- Anything that emits JSONL or CSV of roughly this shape, which is most of
  them, if the command takes a field mapping rather than hardcoding one
  vendor.

The principle this must not break is 1: the format is files, and a converter
is not privileged. An import writes directories and info files that are
byte-for-byte what pidly new would have written, and pidly check passes on
the result with no special case. If that is true, the command is a
convenience over an operation anyone could script — which is 39's test for
whether a command should exist, and this one passes it.

A dry run that prints what it would write, because renumbering 332 tickets
with no way to look first is not something anyone should run twice.

Sequencing: needs 38 to say which beads concepts are load-bearing rather
than merely present, and 6 needs this. Not blocked on 38 — the mapping above
can be decided from the export alone — but worth reading 38's answer before
choosing what to drop.

Worth splitting into children when picked up: the id and renumbering
decision, the beads reader, the generic mapping, and whatever second tool
goes first.

Done when ../idol's 332 beads issues import into a fresh tracker with
dependencies and parentage intact, check passes on the result, closed
tickets are under c/, the old ids are recoverable, and the command reports
every field it could not place rather than discarding it quietly.
